{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And He shuts up the heaven so that there be no rain.</b> This is what He mentioned above: ״למטר השמים תשתה מים״ — the rain of heaven you shall drink water — for this reason He did not give you a land watered like the land of Egypt, so that the irrigation would not be in your hands, and your eyes would always be lifted to Him, for the key of rains is in His hand." "<b>Know that</b> Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua disagree in tractate Ta'anit, chapter one, concerning the question: from where does the rain come? Rabbi Eliezer says: the whole world drinks from the waters of the Ocean, as it is said (Genesis 2:6): ״ואד יעלה מן הארץ והשקה״ — and a mist went up from the earth and watered. Rabbi Yehoshua says: it drinks from the upper waters, as it is said: ״למטר השמים תשתה מים״ — the rain of heaven you shall drink water. And each one has strong proofs. Rabbi Eliezer has a proof from what is written (Psalms 33:7): ״כונס כנד מי הים נותן באוצרות תהומות״ — He gathers the waters of the sea as a heap, puts them in treasuries of the deep — what caused the treasuries to fill? The deep (תהומות); and this is a proof that rain comes from the waters of the Ocean. And as for the fact that the waters of the Ocean are salty while rainwater is sweet — it is sweetened in the clouds. And Rabbi Yehoshua has a proof from what is written (Psalms 104:13): ״משקה הרים מעליותיו״ — He waters the mountains from His upper chambers — and Rabbi Yohanan said: from His upper chambers of above, as it is taught: the upper waters are suspended by the divine utterance, and their fruits are the rainwaters, as it is said (ibid.): ״מפרי מעשיך״ and so forth. The words of these sages are words of the living Elohim, and it is possible that the words of both are confirmed, for sometimes the rain descends from the heavens, that is, from the upper waters, and sometimes from the waters of the Ocean, which are the lower waters. And that which comes from the waters of the Ocean is not called מטר but גשם — from the language of גשמות, meaning physical matter; whereas that which comes from the upper waters is called by both names, in the language of מטר and in the language of גשם. It is called in the language of גשם (Isaiah 55:10): ״כי כאשר ירד הגשם והשלג מן השמים״ — for as the rain and snow come down from the heavens — and it is called in the language of מטר: ״למטר השמים תשתה מים״; (Deuteronomy 28:12): ״את אוצרו הטוב את השמים לתת מטר ארצך בעתו״ and so forth; ״ועצר את השמים ולא יהיה מטר״. And we find in the section of the Flood two expressions, מטר and גשם, as it is said (Genesis 7:4): ״אנכי ממטיר על הארץ״ — I will cause it to rain upon the earth — and it is written (ibid.): ״ויהי הגשם על הארץ״ — and the rain was upon the earth — because there all the fountains of the great deep were burst open, the deep above and the deep below." "<b>And I found</b> in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 5, support for these words, for he said there: the clouds draw from the deeps, as it is said (Psalms 135:7): ״מעלה נשיאים מקצה הארץ״ — He brings up vapors from the ends of the earth — and in every place where the King assigns them, they produce rain, and immediately the earth conceives and sprouts like a widow who conceives through harlotry. But when the Holy One, blessed be He, wishes to bless the produce of the ground, the rain comes from the heavens, which are male waters, and immediately the earth conceives and sprouts seed of blessing, as it is said (Deuteronomy 28:12): ״יפתח ה' לך את אוצרו הטוב את השמים״. Up to here." "<b>And you should be roused</b> regarding the name מטר, which is attributed to the heavens in most places in the Torah, for there is a great difference between the words, and in the distinctions of expression there is wondrous knowledge and glorious wisdom. And set your mind to this: what is it? (Job 37:6): ״וגשם מטר״ — and rain of מטר — for to the snow that is beneath the Throne of Glory He said that it should return to the earth, and to the גשם, which is the body, He said it should return as מטר. And our Sages, of blessed memory, already said: He reversed His flesh into torches of fire, and this is (Psalms 135:7): ״ברקים למטר עשה״ — He made lightning for the מטר — for according to the plain sense of the verse it would follow that lightning should always be seen together with the מטר, but it is not so." "<b>And you shall perish quickly.</b> If, Heaven forbid, you serve idolatry, you shall be exiled from the good land, because the land does not tolerate transgressors, and thus Scripture says (Isaiah 13:9): ״וחטאיה ישמיד ממנה״ — and its sinners He will destroy from it. And our Sages, of blessed memory, compared it to a parable: to what may the matter be likened? To a man who leads his son to a banquet and warns him not to eat too much, so that he may come home clean. The son paid no attention and ate and drank too much; he vomited and filthy-fied all the banqueters. What did the banqueters do? They took him by his hands and his feet and threw him behind the palace."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {3 "The word יחזור appears twice in the Job citation discussion; rendered 'that it should return' both times following the text as written. The antecedent and precise force of each occurrence is somewhat obscure but translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:52.743203763Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}